WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.040
The other day I was exploring options for a&nbsp;
go-wide beatdown deck, so naturally I was on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:11.520
the EDHRec pages of Toski and Tovolar. And on&nbsp;
those pages, I found something that bothered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:20.480
me. On both pages, 60% of decks run Cultivate.&nbsp;
Now, obviously EDHRec has some inherent flaws&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.480
that get further compounded through usage of the&nbsp;
website; it’d be cool if somebody made a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.800
about that or something, but still. 60%. With&nbsp;
a commander that’s low-mana enough not to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.080
boosted out by the ramp part and who draws enough&nbsp;
cards to not need the tacked on land-to-hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.600
You could spend that three mana on literally&nbsp;
anything else. There’s also some questionable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:46.520
draw run in these decks whose commander is an&nbsp;
insanely powerful draw spell. So, what gives?

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.880
Well, I think this ties into a culture of hailing&nbsp;
certain cards as must-plays, one that exists both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:57.240
online and in IRL circles. This sort of effect&nbsp;
has likely always been somewhat the case among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.800
various circles in Magic: the Gathering, since&nbsp;
it’s a social game with an established community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.880
but EDH is especially vulnerable to cycles&nbsp;
of over-hype about particular cards. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.440
is because it’s an even more social format&nbsp;
than the baseline due to having four players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.720
and also, the fact that it’s singleton and&nbsp;
100 cards means that there’s kind of a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.640
of room for fluff in decks and it’ll take&nbsp;
longer for players to identify a mediocre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.680
card than it would in a 60 card format. There&nbsp;
will be the cards that truly should be added&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.360
to most decks on the basis of power—Sol Ring,&nbsp;
Counterspell, Mystic Remora—and you can go check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.920
out my budget cards video if you want my saucy,&nbsp;
saucy opinions about them. But there will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.800
be cards regarded in the same lens but which&nbsp;
truly don’t belong in all or even most decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.600
and I want to focus on a specific category&nbsp;
of cards among these: mana ramp spells.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.680
First, a quick overview. Ramp&nbsp;
makes your amount of mana go up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.160
typically permanently barring interaction&nbsp;
from your opponents. It’s nigh-ubiquitous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.360
in EDH due to the slow speed of the&nbsp;
format, with the first and second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.920
most played nonland cards in the format both&nbsp;
being ramp spells. And don’t get me wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.560
ramp is fantastic and there are lots of great&nbsp;
ramp spells out there. But as you can probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.880
guess by the first couple minutes of this video,&nbsp;
I have opinions about some of them. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.480
I think people gravitate toward certain ones&nbsp;
that are considered “staples”, even when there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:25.160
are other options that are much better suited to&nbsp;
their deck. So, treat this video as a guide—how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.760
to stop jamming Cultivate into every deck, and&nbsp;
figure out what your deck actually wants instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.360
Hopefully there’s plenty of nuance to be gleaned&nbsp;
here, but this is a video principally stemming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.400
from me getting upsetti spaghetti about&nbsp;
Cultivate, so let’s talk about that first.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.480
Cultivate, along with its twin Kodama’s Reach, is&nbsp;
a Rampant Growth that costs an extra mana and has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:51.040
a Lay of the Land attached. It’s a straightforward&nbsp;
formula with some clear appeal in a slow format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.800
and evidently Wizards agreed in 2011—it’s&nbsp;
in all three green precons in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.280
EDH set. This alone likely contributed&nbsp;
to the card’s popularity, and honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.720
the card is right at home in these decks—three&nbsp;
color decks with questionable manabases and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:11.400
healthy spattering of 7-9 mana cards. When you&nbsp;
play Cultivate, you’re actively choosing an extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.920
land to hand rather than the speed of a two mana&nbsp;
ramp spell, and this is absolutely a tradeoff that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.280
some decks want. The more colors you play, the&nbsp;
more that extra land will matter, and colorfixing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.680
might be a valuable goal in its own right if&nbsp;
you’re running a lot of colors with not a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:29.960
of budget. If you’re playing five color and you&nbsp;
have any three colors that include green, a turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:35.960
three Cultivate sets you up for a turn 4 with a&nbsp;
mana of each color available—that’s a great deal!

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.960
This usefulness as a setup piece is most obvious&nbsp;
in a five color deck, but the same logic also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.840
works in a deck with fewer colors—as a general&nbsp;
principle Cultivate is best geared toward decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.880
looking for a pivotal 5 or 6 mana turn. This&nbsp;
brings me to a major consideration when picking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:57.000
out ramp—mana curve. A lot of decks will have one&nbsp;
or more major mana value numbers they care about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:02.400
and your ramp package should be formulated to&nbsp;
fit this. My Glissa deck wants to play Glissa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.560
on turn 2 whenever possible followed up with&nbsp;
more ramp spells, so if we look at a graph of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.560
the mana value of the deck’s ramp spells, it’s&nbsp;
big spikes on 1 and 4 mana, and a smaller spike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.080
on 7 because the deck is a disgusting greed&nbsp;
pile. There are only a few two and three cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:21.640
ramp spells since the deck prefers to skip 2&nbsp;
mana and play Glissa on 3 mana, and the ramp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:27.600
spells of these costs that are in the deck either&nbsp;
create untapped lands or have flexible mana costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.080
When you’re adding a ramp spell to a deck, you&nbsp;
should be asking “what is this ramping toward?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.440
Wanting more lands in general isn’t necessarily&nbsp;
the wrong answer, but if the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:43.040
mana value you’re pushing toward is four, you&nbsp;
shouldn’t run a bunch of three mana ramp spells.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.760
This is part of what prompts my eyebrow raising&nbsp;
when I see 60% of Toski decks running the card.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.600
It doesn’t ramp into the commander, it takes up a&nbsp;
mana slot that could have gone to a card-drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:58.760
beater, and the commander itself encourages volume&nbsp;
of creatures rather than size. This means you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:04.880
probably don’t want that many cards on 5 or 6 mana&nbsp;
other than, I dunno, Mr. Squirrels. The commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:10.360
also draws you a ton of cards, and if your deck&nbsp;
has 33 forests, each of those draws has a healthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:15.160
one third chance of getting you the exact land&nbsp;
you want. You’re playing a mono-colored deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:19.320
and if you play Cultivate you’re effectively&nbsp;
overpaying for ramp that gives you an upside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:25.200
you don’t need. I find Cultivate questionable in&nbsp;
Tovolar for similar reasons—two colors is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.480
few enough that the color fixing probably isn’t&nbsp;
necessary, and werewolves top end consists of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.080
like, a handful of 5 and 6 drops? Rampant&nbsp;
Growth is almost certainly better but I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.960
not sure I’d even go for that over a two&nbsp;
drop beater that can draw you some cards.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.880
Card type is another consideration with regard&nbsp;
to ramp, since artifacts and especially creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.520
will die to removal and board wipes, but land&nbsp;
destruction is much less common. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.080
dorks and rocks are often more efficiently&nbsp;
costed than cards that grab lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.520
so there are tradeoffs to be had. They all&nbsp;
have their place, but the places tend to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:04.800
different. Obviously, for non-green decks, bless&nbsp;
their hearts, you’re mostly stuck with rocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.040
but that doesn’t necessarily mean green decks&nbsp;
will never want rocks. Single green dorks tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.840
to be the most mana-efficient ramp option&nbsp;
at lower budgets, so I tend to go for them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:19.400
in any deck where the 3 cmc slot is important&nbsp;
but the 2 cmc slot is unimportant. My Glissa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:23.560
deck and my Meria deck both have three mana&nbsp;
commanders that they try to land on curve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:28.640
but Glissa runs dorks because it has very few&nbsp;
2 mana plays and Meria runs rocks because the 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:34.080
and 2 mana slots are very important for the deck.&nbsp;
In a third one of my green decks, Scapeshift is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.960
a key piece of the deck, so the deck runs mostly&nbsp;
land-grabbing spells in its ramp suite even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:44.440
they’re slower than single green dorks. Long story&nbsp;
short, different decks will want different things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:49.680
and these are just a handful of examples&nbsp;
of what I ended up on with my green decks.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.560
A final consideration when considering ramp&nbsp;
cards is the net mana cost vs the upfront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:59.640
mana cost. For something like Rampant Growth or&nbsp;
Cultivate, these values are identical since they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:04.520
don’t immediately offer more mana, but some&nbsp;
spells give you untapped lands. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:09.800
Skyshroud Claim has an upfront cost of 4, but&nbsp;
a net cost of 2 if you grab Forests that enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:14.960
untapped. Two ramp spells I generally prefer&nbsp;
to Cultivate in 2 color decks are Harrow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:20.360
Grow from the Ashes, as they grab untapped lands&nbsp;
and thereby allow for more flexible turns than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:26.760
card that grabs tapped lands. If we look at mana&nbsp;
rocks, most theoretically offer mana immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.480
but in practice that can differ by when they’re&nbsp;
played and what deck is playing them. Signets can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:36.920
be a net mana cost of 1 after turn 2 if the&nbsp;
symbols line up, but on 2 mana they can’t do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:42.560
much else. Similarly, colorless-tapping rocks&nbsp;
like Mind Stone add mana immediately if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:48.200
have a place to put one colorless mana, but if&nbsp;
you don’t they’re just the sticker mana cost.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:52.680
I like to think I’ve offered a little toolbox&nbsp;
here in picking out the most appropriate ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.240
and as a final point I’ll address an important&nbsp;
question that I’ve been skirting around so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:01.880
far—that being how much ramp a deck should&nbsp;
run. This question honestly depends on so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:06.880
many factors that I’d feel irresponsible offering&nbsp;
a straightforward answer or even a framework. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:11.680
best way to tell the right amount of your deck&nbsp;
is, well, to play it and see if it feels like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.200
has the right amount of ramp. If you’re ramping&nbsp;
to a particular value, does the deck succeed at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:21.480
that consistently? Does it do so too well, eating&nbsp;
up deck space and leaving you with awkward spare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.800
mana on a lot of turns? There are also differences&nbsp;
in the size of mana ramp spell, so if you find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:31.120
yourself drawing too many ramp spells but want&nbsp;
the same ramping capability, you can cut some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:36.440
small ones for a bigger one or two. Conversely,&nbsp;
you can do the opposite maneuver if your opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:41.880
hands seem consistently loaded with expensive&nbsp;
ramp spells you can’t get to quickly enough.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:47.600
There may very well be strong ramp spells—Elvish&nbsp;
Mystic, Nature’s Lore, Thought Vessel—that don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:53.440
end up being quite what your deck wants, and this&nbsp;
is okay. The best ramp solution isn’t gonna be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:58.280
one size fits all. At the end of the day, what I’m&nbsp;
trying to say here is simply that you should look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:03.720
at your deck’s ramp suite critically. I think when&nbsp;
it comes to the traditionally simpler parts of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:09.800
EDH deck—the lands, the removal, the ramp—a lot&nbsp;
of people have the idea that they can kinda gloss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:14.360
over these things and focus their efforts on the&nbsp;
juicy synergies that comprise the spiritual core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:18.640
of the deck. But these parts are what lets&nbsp;
the core of the deck do its thing smoothly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:23.680
so taking a critical eye toward them will&nbsp;
likely improve your deck more than you think.

